1 | db-architect | Ship the three schema changes that the assistant channel needs, and nothing else. READ FIRST: issue 2799 in full, issue 2797 for the verified facts, docs/operations/migrations.md, docs/operations/migration-filename-gate.md, apps/api/db/CLAUDE.md, and the migration that created the constraints you are changing, apps/api/db/migrations/000033_channel_bindings_and_source.sql. THE THREE CHANGES, each verified as rejected by constraint today. One, the binding table channel column: 000033 line 41 is CHECK channel IN telegram, whatsapp, imessage. Add the assistant channel value. Read the epic and the W0 spec page on the docs branch for the settled name before choosing it, and if W0 has not settled one, use assistant and say so in the PR body. Two, the source columns on conversations and result_sessions: 000033 lines 108 to 119 admit only web, telegram, whatsapp, ios, android. Add the same value to both CHECK constraints. Three, a revision column on result_sessions, integer, not null, default 1, so a later change can make UpdateResultSessionFields refuse a stale writer. Do NOT change the query in this PR; the column is the migration, the predicate is W2 work. HOW: one timestamped migration file per the filename gate, with an honest down migration for each change. The 000033 migration used NOT VALID plus VALIDATE for the source constraints because goose wraps single transactions; read issue 1912 which records that trap and follow whatever it settled on. Regenerate sqlc if any query touches the new column, and paste the real exit code of make sqlc-generate captured directly rather than read after a pipe. Confirm the one-active-binding-per-channel index at 000033 line 81 still holds for the new value, and state in the PR body whether one active binding per assistant host needs a different index shape, without building it. VERIFY: make migrate up and down against a local database, both directions clean, real exit codes; the migration filename gate passes; go build and go vet pass; the existing constraint tests still pass. Open a PR referencing issue 2799 with Refs and no closing keyword, since 2799 has two more parts. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name in commit or PR text, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Do not merge. | feat/ac-w1a-migrations

2 | database-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch feat/ac-w1a-migrations. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: can this migration be applied and reverted on a database that already holds Telegram bindings and web result sessions without losing or rewriting a row. Prove it: in a throwaway database, seed one telegram binding, one web conversation and one web result session, apply the migration, confirm all three rows are untouched and the new value is now accepted, then revert and confirm the rows are still untouched and the new value is rejected again. Paste the exit codes and the row counts. SECOND, confirm the CHECK constraints were widened rather than dropped: a value outside the allowed set must still be refused after the migration. THIRD, confirm the goose single-transaction trap from issue 1912 was handled the way that issue settled, since a NOT VALID constraint inside a wrapped transaction is exactly how 000033 nearly went wrong. FOURTH, confirm the revision column has a default so existing rows are not left null, and that no query was changed in this PR. FIFTH, confirm the filename gate passes and the down migration is real rather than a no-op. SIXTH, confirm no long dash and no vendor name in the diff, commit or PR body. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC ASSISTANT CHANNEL W1A MIGRATIONS, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE and per-finding file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | feat/ac-w1a-migrations
